[
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43234\">#43234</a> shows that, in characteristic 0, the bundle of continuous alternating maps between <code>C^n</code> vector bundles is <code>C^n</code>. I was surprised that I needed characteristic 0: we have already the bundle as a continuous bundle, and no characteristic assumption is needed there. Fortunately, Bourbaki makes the same assumption (although he never says where it's needed, because it's a book of results without proofs), so it's probably reasonable.</p>",
        "id": 620949055,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1788341661
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Of course, it's too big for a single PR, so I've split a few prerequisites in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43338\">#43338</a>, about smoothness results for different maps related to alternating maps, in vector spaces (+300, -9). I can split it further if it helps.</p>",
        "id": 620949399,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1788341742
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's an interesting exercise to see what breaks up in positive characteristic. To say that the bundle is analytic means that the coordinate changes should be analytic. It boils down to the following: consider the precomposition by a linear map, on spaces of alternating maps (in Lean terms, <code>(E →L[𝕜] F) → (F [⋀^ι]→L[𝕜] G) →L[𝕜] (E [⋀^ι]→L[𝕜] G)</code>, mapping <code>f</code> to the map mapping <code>m (v_1, ..., v_i)</code> to <code>m (f v_1, ...., f v_i)</code>). Is this map analytic as a function of <code>f</code>? I can not prove it in positive characteristic when the spaces are not finite-dimensional.</p>",
        "id": 620956521,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1788343279
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>With AI assistance (and some insight from <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"387244\">@Yaël Dillies</span>), I was able to prove the necessary lemma without the <code>[CharZero]</code> assumption in all but the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>ω</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ω</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> case: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43548\">#43548</a></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">contDiff_compContinuousLinearMapCLM</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"bp\">∞</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ContDiff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">compContinuousLinearMapCLM</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">⋀^</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">⋀^</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>However one step of the proof relies on induction on the smoothness <code>n</code>, which is why the proof does not extend to the analytic case. Specifically, the following lemma is by induction:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">comp_contDiff_iff</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">uE</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uF</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uG</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uE</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uF</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">uG</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→ₗᵢ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hΦ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsClosed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">range</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"bp\">∞</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ContDiff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ContDiff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 622342535,
        "sender_full_name": "Jack McCarthy",
        "timestamp": 1788803222
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think it is not very surprising that the result holds in the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\neq\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ω</span></span></span></span> case, precisely because of this lemma (which I noticed we were lacking while reviewing Sebastien’s PR, and which should be generalized to a linear <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsClosedEmbedding#doc\">docs#IsClosedEmbedding</a>), which is not clearly true in postitive-characteristic for analytic functions. The interesting question is really here.</p>\n<p>Also it is a bit surprising to me that you would prove this by induction: I think with the right API this should follow directly from the definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=HasFTaylorSeriesUpTo#doc\">docs#HasFTaylorSeriesUpTo</a>.</p>\n<p>Note that I am not writing these comments for you to feed them to an LLM; so please don’t.</p>",
        "id": 622349418,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1788805711
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that in positive characteristic the only interesting case is analytic, because otherwise manifolds are badly behaved.</p>",
        "id": 622350905,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1788806291
    }
]